#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119796 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e460dcd-59cf-54d2-80e6-c0d72b6bafdd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using Two or More Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18cb4e22-4527-5cdf-a246-1015081dac6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241398} {:answer/id #uuid "45ff70ac-0687-5b4f-8a03-e3801fde7dde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241811} {:answer/id #uuid "c2e14ccb-77c2-5ea4-bf3e-93ea6b71314d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242893} {:answer/id #uuid "c7019515-4ac5-5e22-b2ba-750fda287b5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242938} {:answer/id #uuid "f01bc462-8fff-5df6-8acb-52fca6d667fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f01bc462-8fff-5df6-8acb-52fca6d667fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243312} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d43a551-8858-5c8b-93c4-57c4ba597de0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54845a6f-21d5-55bc-a564-7e1e09132e6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106855" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -8 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{23}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -8 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{31}=4,{u}_{23}=2,$ and ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{23}=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119796 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e460dcd-59cf-54d2-80e6-c0d72b6bafdd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using Two or More Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006f8bad-4488-5247-b030-e69c65d69aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241189} {:answer/id #uuid "5c0029bc-5dd1-5f3b-a321-0734ae89305c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242011} {:answer/id #uuid "c5338b5d-9d5a-53f8-8757-89e53bce6a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242918} {:answer/id #uuid "cb3972a3-507c-51c5-a785-eebe4b438e06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242969} {:answer/id #uuid "de80bb7f-5c83-554e-90e1-3ead07f6bbb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c0029bc-5dd1-5f3b-a321-0734ae89305c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242011} :answer-field/id #uuid "27b176de-565d-5931-ae28-b4d1ef631a50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b30a7824-ca9d-5133-93d0-3fa15525aa26" :question/math-academy-id "q-106858" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{3}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{2}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{3},$ ${E}_{2},$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & ={E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}{E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}{E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-1,$ ${u}_{33}=4,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{33}=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119796 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e460dcd-59cf-54d2-80e6-c0d72b6bafdd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using Two or More Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0432df21-0cbf-5310-b63d-d6d35b252ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299948} {:answer/id #uuid "0e624142-c08f-57f0-ac81-3c8acbe9f224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300009} {:answer/id #uuid "934f2563-3041-51a5-be22-78f3bc46ae92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300701} {:answer/id #uuid "ac425112-4037-539d-b950-dc90094cf5b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300823} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c403d6-3218-5d7c-bb7b-d08ac835de9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e624142-c08f-57f0-ac81-3c8acbe9f224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300009} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0579342-0bba-5c5a-a576-6b032097c4cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "912ad134-e874-55d5-a192-6e414d5ad134" :question/math-academy-id "q-32533" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-0.5){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=0.5,{u}_{33}=1,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=0.5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c91fb2b-5607-555d-b897-f0e76765dfa6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lower Triangular Matrix Given the Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f515a37-0532-5379-b6c5-60cae121cd10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379388} {:answer/id #uuid "0f26d137-daef-5ba0-b02f-8ef0beb9e1f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379389} {:answer/id #uuid "1c8445fa-f6b7-5338-b384-7962fded25ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379390} {:answer/id #uuid "88a45256-4352-5161-b66d-e81037757768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379391} {:answer/id #uuid "1acc907a-8f9c-5345-b778-5b939debd5f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88a45256-4352-5161-b66d-e81037757768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379391} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebcf7832-5de6-5a02-b8c1-346ae0bc9869" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a83c5862-ce81-57be-a501-0eb1330527bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-32681" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-5{R}_{1}\n$$\n\nGiven that $C$ has the LU factorization $C=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-5)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ C & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
